;;; 00e-standard-library.slat — Motoi Scheme Reference · Chapter 00e ·
;;; The standard library: parts you can include, exclude, and inspect.
;;;
;;; Written 2026-07-24, the day the standard was locked. This chapter is the
;;; programmer's tour of Motoi's standard library. It follows 00d-loading
;;; (eager vs lazy) and belongs to the intro: read it once and you know what
;;; the language ships with, where each verb comes from, and how to add your
;;; own part the same way the standard parts were added. Applies to Motoi,
;;; which inherits the same parts machinery.

(dialect :applies ("motoi") :note "Standard-library parts chapter — the reference audience is programmers, 11 to 111.")

(chapter :number "00e" :library "standard-library" :training-eligible #t
  :confidentiality "public"
  :read-order-hint "read after 00d-loading.slat, before 01-core.slat"
  :prose
"# The standard library — parts you can include, exclude, and inspect

## Who this chapter is for

This is the reference. The reference is for **programmers** — every one of them,
from an eleven-year-old writing a first loop to a hundred-and-eleven-year-old who
has written more code than you ever will. It tells you what the language gives
you, what each verb does, and how to reach for it. It does not tell you how the
runtime is *built* — that is a different book (the Scheme Engineering manual, for
people rebuilding or reasoning about the language from a language-engineering and
computer-science angle). Here, you are a programmer. You want to write a program.
This chapter tells you what is already in the box and how to add more.

## What Motoi ships with

Motoi's standard library is **R7RS-small plus a small set of named SRFI parts**.
That is the whole standard. No R6RS, no Racket extensions, nobody else's dialect.

- **R7RS-small** is the core — the official small-Scheme standard, the semantic
  authority. When a name like `map`, `cons`, `string-length`, or `assoc` means
  one thing in standard Scheme, it means exactly that thing here. This is the
  promise that lets anyone who already knows Scheme sit down and start typing.
- **SRFI parts** add the extra libraries the community standardized: lists,
  strings, hash tables, random numbers. Each SRFI is its own **part** — a named,
  numbered unit you can include or leave out.

The parts, as of the 2026-07-24 lock:

| part       | what it gives you            | mode  | in the box by default? |
|------------|------------------------------|-------|------------------------|
| R7RS-small | the core language            | eager | yes                    |
| SRFI-1     | the list library             | eager | yes                    |
| SRFI-13    | the string library           | lazy  | pulled in when needed  |
| SRFI-125   | intermediate hash tables     | lazy  | pulled in when needed  |
| SRFI-27    | sources of random bits       | lazy  | pulled in when needed  |

**The default bundle** is R7RS-small + SRFI-1. Those two are always on — the
\"expected superset\" every program can assume. The other three load lazily: their
verbs are there when you touch them, and they cost nothing until you do. (If you
have read chapter 00d, this is the same eager/lazy story, applied to the standard
parts.)

## Why the names match standard Scheme — the conformance ladder

Every verb in the standard library is held to the **highest standard it matches**,
in this order:

1. **R7RS-small first.** If the standard defines it, we use the standard name,
   the standard signature, the standard behavior. Full stop.
2. **Then a SRFI.** If R7RS-small does not cover it but a SRFI does, we conform to
   the SRFI.
3. **Then Motoi-own** — only if nothing standard matches. `random`, for example,
   is not in R7RS-small, so it follows SRFI-27.

This is why you will not find a verb here that *looks* like standard Scheme but
behaves differently. A verb that duplicated a standard one under a twisted name
or a different argument order would be a bug, and we fix bugs. One example worth
knowing: **folding**. Motoi uses SRFI-1's `fold` and `fold-right`, where the
element comes first and the accumulator second — `(fold cons '() lst)`. It does
**not** ship R6RS's `fold-left` or Racket's `foldl`/`foldr`. If you learned fold
somewhere else and the argument order surprises you, that is the conformance
ladder doing its job — see the broken-examples note at the end of this chapter.

## Every verb tells you where it came from

Open any standard part's spec and each verb carries its **provenance**: which
standard it belongs to and which section. `string-upcase` says SRFI-13.
`hash-table-ref` says SRFI-125. `list-tabulate` says SRFI-1. Nothing in the
standard library is anonymous. If you ever wonder \"is this real Scheme or did
Motoi make it up?\", the provenance answers you.

## Looking at the library from inside your program

The language can show you its own standard library. These verbs are always on:

```scheme
(std-parts)                 ; every standard part, with its metadata
(lib-info 'SRFI-13)         ; one part's metadata (name, spec, mode, verb count)
(lib-verbs 'SRFI-1)         ; the list of verb names a part provides
(lib-validate 'SRFI-125)    ; run the 5-point gate on one part, get a report
(lib-validate-all)          ; run the gate on every standard part
```

`(lib-info 'SRFI-13)` comes back looking like this:

```scheme
((:name \"SRFI-13\") (:std \"SRFI-13\") (:spec \"spec/srfi-13.slatl\")
 (:mode lazy) (:verb-count 15))
```

## Looking at it from the shell

The same information is a shell command away:

```
$ motoi lib list
R7RS-small   eager  115 verbs   spec/r7rs-small.slatl
SRFI-1       eager   42 verbs   spec/srfi-1.slatl
SRFI-13      lazy    27 verbs   spec/srfi-13.slatl
SRFI-125     lazy    27 verbs   spec/srfi-125.slatl
SRFI-27      lazy     4 verbs   spec/srfi-27.slatl

$ motoi lib info SRFI-13       # one part
$ motoi lib validate          # check every part against the running language
```

## Writing your own part — the same door the standard parts came through

Here is the good part: the standard parts are not special. They passed through a
gate, and *anything you write can pass through the same gate*. Write a library,
run the register command on it, and if it clears the gate it installs. If it does
not clear the gate, **nothing installs** — a broken part cannot dirty the language.

```
$ motoi lib register path/to/my-part.js
```

Registration is a CLI operation, not an in-language one. There is no
`register-lib` verb: loading a part means importing a JavaScript module, which
is asynchronous, and a Scheme verb that returned a pending import would hand
you a value you cannot use. The command above is the whole interface.

For a part to be accepted, its file must declare the same things every standard
part declares:

- `STD` — the part's name and provenance (e.g. \"SRFI-42\", or your own name).
- `SPEC` — the path to a spec file kept alongside the part.
- `VERBS` — the list of verb names it provides.
- `LAZY` — `true` or `false`: does it load on demand, or at boot?
- an `install…` function — the code that actually defines the verbs.

## The five-point gate — what \"accepted\" means

When you register a part, the language runs five checks. All five must pass:

```
$ motoi lib validate
PASS  SRFI-13
      ✓ 1-bucket:  part \"SRFI-13\" declares 27 verbs
      ✓ 2-spec:    spec present at spec/srfi-13.slatl
      ✓ 4-toggle:  LAZY=true (lazy, explicit)
      ✓ 3-verbs:   all 27 verbs bind to functions
      ✓ 5-runs:    installed surface is live (all verbs callable)
```

In plain words:

1. **Own bucket + provenance.** It is a real, named part with verbs, and every
   verb says where it came from.
2. **Spec kept alongside.** Its spec file exists on disk, next to the part.
3. **Every verb implemented.** Each verb the part promised actually binds to a
   working function.
4. **Includable / excludable.** It declares whether it is eager or lazy, and
   nothing else secretly depends on it being always on.
5. **It runs.** The installed surface is live — the verbs are callable, for real.

A part that fails any check is rejected, and the report tells you which check
failed, so you can fix it and try again. This is the whole promise: *write a lib,
run this on it, and it installs itself — but only if it is honest.*

## Broken on purpose — the teaching lane

You will find a folder of **broken examples** in the reference
(`scheme/reference/broken-examples/`). These are programs that do **not** run,
kept on purpose. Each one shows a mistake a Scheme programmer coming from another
dialect would naturally make — `fold-left` with the accumulator first (that is
R6RS, not us), `hash-table-exists?` for a membership test (that is the SRFI-69
spelling; the standard here is SRFI-125, which calls it `hash-table-contains?`) —
and then explains why it is wrong here, what the right Motoi way is, and how three
other Scheme libraries would each spell the same idea. They are not failures to be
cleaned up. They are the map of the differences between R5RS, R6RS, the SRFIs, and
Motoi. If you ever get a surprise, there is a good chance the surprise is already
written down there with its lesson attached.

## The short version

- The standard is R7RS-small + a few named SRFI parts. Nothing else.
- R7RS-small and SRFI-1 are always on. SRFI-13, SRFI-125, SRFI-27, SRFI-133 load when you
  reach for them.
- Standard names behave the standard way, always — that is the conformance ladder.
- `(std-parts)`, `(lib-info …)`, `motoi lib list` let you look at the library.
- `motoi lib register` lets you add your own part through the same five-point
  gate the standard parts passed. It is a CLI command; there is no verb form.
- Broken examples are kept to teach the differences between dialects. Read them."

  :verbs (
    (:name "std-parts"        :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(std-parts) -> list-of-alists"
     :summary "Every standard-library part (R7RS-small + adopted SRFIs) as a list of metadata alists."
     :example "(length (std-parts)) ; => 5")
    (:name "lib-info"         :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-info part-name-symbol) -> alist | #f"
     :summary "One standard part's metadata alist (name, std, spec, mode, verb-count), or #f if it is not a standard part."
     :example "(lib-info 'SRFI-13) ; => ((:name \"SRFI-13\") (:std \"SRFI-13\") (:spec \"spec/srfi-13.slatl\") (:mode lazy) (:verb-count 27))")
    (:name "lib-verbs"        :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-verbs part-name-symbol) -> list-of-symbols | #f"
     :summary "The list of verb names a part provides, as symbols, or #f if it is not a standard part."
     :example "(lib-verbs 'SRFI-27) ; => (random-integer random-real ...)")
    (:name "lib-validate"     :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-validate part-name-symbol) -> report-alist | #f"
     :summary "Run the 5-point gate on one standard part against the live language; return a report alist with :ok and per-check results."
     :example "(lib-validate 'SRFI-125) ; => ((:ok #t) (:std \"SRFI-125\") ...)")
    (:name "lib-validate-all" :library "standard-library"
     :signature "(lib-validate-all) -> list-of-reports"
     :summary "Run the 5-point gate on every standard part; one report per part."
     :example "(lib-validate-all) ; => one report per standard part")
    (:name "register-lib"     :library "standard-library"
     :status "needs-alfred"
     :status-note "DRIFT (re-verified 2026-08-02, still open): `register-lib` is NOT wired as a Scheme verb in the `run` env — `(register-lib \"lib/std/srfi-27.js\")` reports 'unbound symbol: register-lib'. It exists only as the JS function `registerLib` (src/lib-register.js:54); the neighbouring lib-info / lib-verbs / lib-validate / lib-validate-all ARE wired. DECISION NEEDED: (a) wire a `register-lib` Scheme verb over registerLib, or (b) reword this entry as a JS-API note rather than an in-language verb. The example does not run as written."
     :signature "(register-lib file-path-string) -> report-alist"
     :summary "Validate a candidate part file against the 5-point gate and, if it passes, install its verbs into the language. A part that fails installs nothing."
     :example "(register-lib \"lib/std/srfi-27.js\") ; => report with :ok #t")))

;; ═══ 5 verbs in standard-library introspection ═══

(verb
  :name "std-parts" :std "Motoi"
  :library "standard-library"
  :kind "io"
  :signature "(std-parts) -> list-of-alists"
  :summary "Every standard-library part as a metadata alist: name, std, spec path, mode, verb count."
  :provenance "lib/system/lib-tools.js + core/parts-registry.js; Motoi-own. Read 2026-08-02."
  :explanation "std-parts is the language looking at its own standard library. It returns one alist per adopted part, in the canonical registry order — R7RS-small, SRFI-1, SRFI-13, SRFI-125, SRFI-27, SRFI-133 — and each alist has exactly five entries: (:name string), (:std string), (:spec string), (:mode symbol), (:verb-count integer). The mode is the symbol eager or lazy, taken from the part's own LAZY declaration, so it is the truth about loading rather than a comment about it. The verb count is the length of the part's declared VERBS list, not a count of everything the part happens to bind. Nothing is computed live: this is the registry table, read out."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(length (std-parts))" :note "7 — the whole standard is seven parts, and that number is the lock.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':name p))) (std-parts))" :note "(\"R7RS-small\" \"SRFI-1\" \"SRFI-13\" \"SRFI-125\" \"SRFI-27\" \"SRFI-48\" \"SRFI-133\") — registry order, core first; the names are strings, not symbols.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(apply + (map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))) (std-parts)))" :note "300 — the declared size of the entire standard library, summed across parts. It grows as parts do, so read the number the call gives you rather than this one.")
  )
  :caveats ("The verb count is what the part DECLARES in its VERBS list, not a count of live bindings. A part that binds a helper it never declared does not show up here." "The order is registry order, not alphabetical. Do not sort it and then assume the first entry is still the core." "Each entry is an alist of two-element LISTS, not pairs — reach in with (cadr (assq ':name p)), not (cdr (assq ':name p))." "The counts drift as parts grow. Any prose that hard-codes them goes stale; ask the verb instead.")
  :drawbacks ("No dependency information — the table says what the parts are, not which ones need which." "No indication of whether a lazy part has actually been loaded yet in this session; mode is a declaration, not a state." "Returns a fresh list every call, so it is not cheap in a loop.")
  :usecases ("Printing a startup banner that names the standard the program is running against." "Checking that a build has the parts a program expects before the program touches them." "Generating documentation tables from the live registry instead of hand-maintaining them." "Answering 'how big is the standard library' with a number nobody had to count.")
  :related ("lib-info" "lib-verbs" "lib-validate" "lib-validate-all" "available-libs")
  :learn (:concept "A language that can name its own parts can be audited by a program instead of by a person reading a table." :prerequisites ("assq") :progression "Learn std-parts for the whole table, lib-info for one row, lib-verbs for the contents of a row.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (part-line p)\n  (list (cadr (assq ':name p)) (cadr (assq ':mode p)) (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))))\n(display (map part-line (std-parts))) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (counts) (map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))) (std-parts)))\n(display (= (apply + (counts)) (apply + (counts)))) (newline)\n(display (apply + (counts))) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (eager-parts)\n  (let loop ((xs (std-parts)) (acc '()))\n    (cond ((null? xs) (reverse acc))\n          ((eq? (cadr (assq ':mode (car xs))) 'eager)\n           (loop (cdr xs) (cons (cadr (assq ':name (car xs))) acc)))\n          (else (loop (cdr xs) acc)))))\n(display (eager-parts)) (newline)"
     :problem "A program written against one build of Motoi is being run on another, and a string verb that used to be there is unbound. The audit question is not 'which verb' — it is 'which PART', and whether the part is present at all or merely lazy and never touched. std-parts answers the first half of that in one call, and the answer is a table a program can check rather than a paragraph a person has to trust."
     :explanation "eng: flatten each part to the three fields an operator actually reads — what it is called, when it loads, how big it is. math: the sum is stable across calls, so the number can be logged and compared between builds. cs: filter to the eager parts, which is the set a program may assume is live before it has called anything."
     :meta "Why ask the registry rather than read the chapter: the chapter is prose written on a date, and this very chapter's prose has already drifted from the table it describes. Why the mode field matters more than the count: a lazy part is present in the registry and absent from the environment until something touches it, so a presence check that only looks at names will pass on a build where the part cannot actually load. The tell that this went wrong is an unbound-symbol error for a verb that std-parts happily lists.")
    (:eng "Log the whole table at startup for any program that will run on more than one build. It is five lines and it dates the run."
     :math "The sum over parts is a size metric; compare it between builds, do not memorize it."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a capability manifest — the runtime publishing its own feature table. Related: feature detection, ABI version records, package lock files."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The self-describing catalogue: a system that carries a machine-readable list of its own contents, so nobody has to maintain a separate list that can disagree with it."
     :rungs ((:domain "standard-library (the parts registry naming every adopted part and its size)" :code "(display (map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':name p))) (std-parts))) (newline)")
             (:domain "library shelf (a card catalogue generated from the shelves rather than typed once and left)" :code "(define shelf (list (list 'poetry 12) (list 'maps 4)))\n(display (map car shelf)) (newline)\n(display (apply + (map cadr shelf))) (newline)")
             (:domain "set theory (a family of sets described by its index, so the family's size is derived, not asserted)" :code "(define family (list 3 5 9))\n(display (length family)) (newline)\n(display (apply + family)) (newline)"))
     :explanation "The registry, the generated catalogue, and the indexed family all share one shape: the description is DERIVED from the thing, so it cannot drift from it. The failure mode they all avoid is the hand-typed list — a card catalogue kept separately from the shelves goes wrong the first time a book moves, and a prose table of verb counts goes wrong the first time a verb is added."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a description and a described thing are stored separately. Where it does not transfer: when the description is the AUTHORITY and the thing is supposed to conform to it — a specification is not a generated catalogue, and generating it from the implementation would destroy exactly the property that makes it useful.")
    (:eng "Generate inventories, never type them. A typed inventory has a shelf life measured in commits."
     :math "Derivation preserves agreement; assertion does not."
     :cs "The CS analog is reflection — the program reading its own structure instead of a duplicate description of it."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (length (std-parts))) (newline)\n(display (map (lambda (p) (length p)) (std-parts))) (newline)"
     :math-program "(display (equal? (std-parts) (std-parts))) (newline)\n(display (= (apply + (map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))) (std-parts))) 215)) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (has-all-five? p)\n  (and (assq ':name p) (assq ':std p) (assq ':spec p) (assq ':mode p) (assq ':verb-count p) #t))\n(display (map has-all-five? (std-parts))) (newline)"
     :claim "std-parts returns one entry per adopted part, every part carries exactly the same five keys, and the table is stable within a session — two calls are equal?."
     :invariant "|std-parts()| = 5 ∧ ∀p ∈ std-parts(). keys(p) = {:name, :std, :spec, :mode, :verb-count} ∧ std-parts() = std-parts()"
     :meta "The pattern is a uniform record shape proved by construction: every alist is built by one function over one registry array, so heterogeneity is impossible unless the registry itself is malformed. What falsifies the naive reading: nothing here proves the COUNTS are right — verb-count reports what a part declared, and a part that declares a verb it does not bind still reports it. That check is lib-validate's job, not this one's. See the 5-point gate in 00e prose."
    )
    (:eng "Smoke test: shape is uniform and the table is stable, which is what lets a caller index it positionally."
     :math "Equality of two calls establishes purity over the session; it says nothing across sessions."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a pure accessor over immutable configuration."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (banner)\n  (map (lambda (p)\n         (list (cadr (assq ':name p))\n               (cadr (assq ':mode p))\n               (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))))\n       (std-parts)))\n(display (banner)) (newline)\n(display (apply + (map caddr (banner)))) (newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (partition-by-mode)\n  (let loop ((xs (std-parts)) (e '()) (l '()))\n    (cond ((null? xs) (list (reverse e) (reverse l)))\n          ((eq? (cadr (assq ':mode (car xs))) 'eager)\n           (loop (cdr xs) (cons (cadr (assq ':name (car xs))) e) l))\n          (else (loop (cdr xs) e (cons (cadr (assq ':name (car xs))) l))))))\n(display (partition-by-mode)) (newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (audit)\n  (map (lambda (p)\n         (let ((n (cadr (assq ':name p))))\n           (list n (cadr (assq ':verb-count p)) (length (lib-verbs n)))))\n       (std-parts)))\n(display (audit)) (newline)"
     :base "std-parts hands back a five-row table of the standard library."
     :emergent "a conformance census — every part cross-checked against its own declared verb list, so a registry whose count disagrees with its contents is caught by a program rather than by a reader"
     :meta "The composition joins std-parts against lib-verbs on the name field, which is the classic two-table join: one table says how many, the other says which ones, and the interesting output is where they disagree. Same family: a package manifest checked against the files on disk, a database row count checked against its index, a shipping manifest checked against the container. In book-of-composition this is the reconcile-two-sources shape — the value is never in either table alone, it is in the difference.")
    (:eng "Cross-check declared counts against declared contents. A count that nothing verifies is a rumour."
     :math "The join is on the name key; the interesting set is the symmetric difference, not the intersection."
     :cs "The CS pattern is manifest reconciliation, the same check a package installer runs before it trusts a lockfile.")))

(verb
  :name "lib-info" :std "Motoi"
  :library "standard-library"
  :kind "io"
  :signature "(lib-info part-name-symbol) -> alist | #f"
  :summary "One standard part's metadata alist — name, std, spec path, mode, verb count — or #f if the name is not a standard part."
  :provenance "lib/system/lib-tools.js:65; findPart in core/parts-registry.js:66. Motoi-own. Read 2026-08-02; miss value corrected from JavaScript null to #f the same day."
  :explanation "lib-info is one row of the std-parts table, looked up by name. The lookup tries an exact match first and then falls back to a case-insensitive one, so (lib-info 'srfi-13) and (lib-info 'SRFI-13) return the same row. The argument may be a symbol or a string; both are stringified before the match. The row is the same five-entry alist std-parts produces: (:name string), (:std string), (:spec string), (:mode symbol), (:verb-count integer). A miss is #f, so a bare conditional is a correct guard; pair? is equally correct and says more about what you expect back. Until 2026-08-02 the miss was a JavaScript null that crossed into Scheme as a TRUTHY value, which made (if (lib-info 'nope) …) take the true branch — that is fixed, and the caveats below record what the trap looked like."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(cadr (assq ':verb-count (lib-info 'SRFI-13)))" :note "27 — the live count, which is not the number this chapter's prose still quotes.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(equal? (lib-info 'srfi-13) (lib-info 'SRFI-13))" :note "#t — the name lookup falls back to a case-insensitive match.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(if (lib-info 'no-such-part) 'truthy 'falsy)" :note "falsy — a miss is #f, so the ordinary conditional is a correct guard.")
  )
  :caveats ("A miss is #f, NOT the empty list. (null? (lib-info 'nope)) is #f, because #f and '() are different objects in Scheme." "Before 2026-08-02 a miss was a JavaScript null, which is truthy in Motoi — code written against that engine may still carry a pair? guard where a bare conditional now reads better. The pair? guard is still correct; it was never wrong, only lonely." "Case-insensitivity is a FALLBACK, not the rule: an exact match wins first. If two parts ever differ only by case, the exact spelling decides and the other is unreachable by the loose one." "The :mode entry is a symbol, not a string. Compare it with eq? against 'eager or 'lazy, not with string=?." "The verb count is declared, not measured. lib-validate is what checks the declaration against reality.")
  :drawbacks ("No error detail on a miss — you cannot distinguish a typo from a part that was deliberately excluded from this build." "#f is a fine answer to 'is there a part called X' and a poor one to 'why not', which is the usual cost of a boolean-shaped failure." "It only knows STANDARD parts. A library that loaded through require is invisible here; that is available-libs' territory.")
  :usecases ("Reading one part's verb count without pulling the whole table." "Checking whether a part is eager before assuming its verbs are live." "Resolving a user-typed part name in any casing." "Locating a part's spec file on disk from inside a program.")
  :related ("std-parts" "lib-verbs" "lib-validate" "available-libs" "require")
  :learn (:concept "A lookup that can miss must say so in a value the caller can test; a miss that tests as success is a bug the caller inherits." :prerequisites ("std-parts" "assq") :progression "Learn std-parts for the table, lib-info for a row, then lib-validate to check the row against the running language.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (part? name) (pair? (lib-info name)))\n(display (part? 'SRFI-1)) (newline)\n(display (part? 'SRFI-42)) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (info-count name)\n  (if (pair? (lib-info name)) (cadr (assq ':verb-count (lib-info name))) 0))\n(display (info-count 'SRFI-27)) (newline)\n(display (info-count 'nope)) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (safe-info name)\n  (let ((i (lib-info name)))\n    (if (pair? i) i (list (list ':name \"unknown\") (list ':verb-count 0)))))\n(display (cadr (assq ':name (safe-info 'SRFI-13)))) (newline)\n(display (cadr (assq ':name (safe-info 'nope)))) (newline)"
     :problem "A setup script checks that a part is present before using it, the check passes, and the very next line fails with an unbound symbol. The audit question is what the check actually tested. On the engine before 2026-08-02 it tested truthiness against a miss value that was truthy, so the check could never have failed. Every one of the three programs asks pair? instead, which separates a row from a miss no matter which value the miss happens to be — and that is why all three still read correctly now that the miss is #f."
     :explanation "eng: a named predicate, so the guard is written once instead of at every call site. math: a total function — the miss maps to zero rather than to an error, which is what a sum over parts wants. cs: a default row, so downstream code that expects five keys always gets five keys."
     :meta "Why pair? and not null?: (null? (lib-info 'nope)) is #f, because the miss is #f and #f is not the empty list. Why pair? rather than the bare conditional even now: it states the shape you expect, so it keeps working if the miss value changes again — which it did once already. The tell that the old version had gone wrong was a guard that had never once taken its false branch, which is invisible until the day it needed to.")
    (:eng "Wrap the pair? check in a named predicate the first time you need it. A raw lib-info in a conditional is a defect waiting for a typo."
     :math "Make the lookup total: choose a value for the miss and return it, so callers do not have to branch."
     :cs "The CS pattern is null-object substitution — replace the absent value with a well-formed empty one at the boundary."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The lookup that must be able to say no: a query whose 'not found' answer has to be distinguishable, by the ordinary test, from a found one."
     :rungs ((:domain "standard-library (a part lookup whose miss is #f, so both the bare conditional and pair? agree)" :code "(display (if (lib-info 'nope) 'truthy 'falsy)) (newline)\n(display (pair? (lib-info 'nope))) (newline)")
             (:domain "hotel front desk (a clerk who says 'certainly' to every room number, including the ones that do not exist)" :code "(define rooms (list 101 102))\n(define (has? n) (if (memv n rooms) #t #f))\n(display (has? 101)) (newline)\n(display (has? 999)) (newline)")
             (:domain "partial functions (a function undefined on part of its domain, given a junk value there instead of a signal)" :code "(define (safe-div a b) (if (= b 0) 'undefined (/ a b)))\n(display (safe-div 6 3)) (newline)\n(display (safe-div 6 0)) (newline)"))
     :explanation "The hotel clerk and the partial function show the failure this lookup used to have: the caller asks a yes-or-no question and gets back something that answers yes no matter what. The fix is identical in each — make the absent case a value the ordinary test can distinguish, which for Scheme means #f, and check it at the edge rather than hoping it propagates."
     :meta "The shape transfers to every lookup API. Where it stops: when the absent case is genuinely a legitimate value of the result type — a hash table storing #f as a real value cannot use #f as its miss signal either, which is exactly why SRFI-125's ref takes a failure thunk instead of guessing. lib-info is safe from that because no part's metadata is ever #f.")
    (:eng "Test the miss path explicitly the first time you write the guard. A guard whose false branch is never exercised is not a guard."
     :math "A partial function needs a distinguished bottom, not a plausible-looking element of the codomain."
     :cs "The CS analog is the null-versus-absent distinction — the reason option types and sentinel-free APIs exist."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (length (lib-info 'SRFI-125))) (newline)\n(display (cadr (assq ':spec (lib-info 'SRFI-125)))) (newline)"
     :math-program "(display (equal? (lib-info 'srfi-27) (lib-info 'SRFI-27))) (newline)\n(display (equal? (lib-info 'SRFI-27) (lib-info 'SRFI-27))) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (pair? (lib-info 'SRFI-1))) (newline)\n(display (pair? (lib-info 'nope))) (newline)\n(display (if (lib-info 'nope) 'truthy 'falsy)) (newline)\n(display (null? (lib-info 'nope))) (newline)"
     :claim "A hit is a five-entry alist; the lookup is case-insensitive and stable; and a miss is #f, which is falsy, is not a pair, and is not the empty list."
     :invariant "hit(n) ⇒ |lib-info(n)| = 5 ∧ lib-info(lower(n)) = lib-info(n) ∧ ¬hit(n) ⇒ r = #f ∧ ¬pair?(r) ∧ ¬null?(r)"
     :meta "The pattern is a proof that pins down the miss value rather than only the hit: the cs-program prints #t, #f, falsy, #f, so the miss is shown to be exactly #f and not the empty list. What falsifies the naive reading: the fourth line. A reader who assumed the miss was '() would predict #t there and get #f. Idempotence and case-folding are proved by construction — findPart does an exact scan, then a lowercased scan, over a frozen array."
    )
    (:eng "Smoke test: hit shape, case-folding, and the exact value of the miss, all three visible in one run."
     :math "Two lookups of the same name are equal?; this establishes purity, not correctness of the contents."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a linear scan over immutable configuration with a case-folded fallback."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (part-report names)\n  (map (lambda (n)\n         (if (pair? (lib-info n))\n             (list n 'present (cadr (assq ':mode (lib-info n))))\n             (list n 'absent 'none)))\n       names))\n(display (part-report (list 'SRFI-1 'SRFI-42 'SRFI-13))) (newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (declared names)\n  (apply + (map (lambda (n)\n                  (if (pair? (lib-info n)) (cadr (assq ':verb-count (lib-info n))) 0))\n                names)))\n(display (declared (list 'SRFI-1 'SRFI-13))) (newline)\n(display (declared (list 'SRFI-1 'nope))) (newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (require-parts names)\n  (let loop ((xs names) (missing '()))\n    (cond ((null? xs) (if (null? missing) 'ok (list 'missing (reverse missing))))\n          ((pair? (lib-info (car xs))) (loop (cdr xs) missing))\n          (else (loop (cdr xs) (cons (car xs) missing))))))\n(display (require-parts (list 'SRFI-1 'SRFI-13))) (newline)\n(display (require-parts (list 'SRFI-1 'SRFI-42 'nope))) (newline)"
     :base "lib-info answers one question about one part."
     :emergent "a dependency preflight — a program stating the parts it needs, checking all of them at once, and naming every one that is missing before it runs a line of real work"
     :meta "The composition maps a single-row lookup over a list of names and partitions on the pair? guard. What emerges is the precondition-check family: declare requirements as data, verify them all in one pass, report the complete failure set. Same family: a Makefile's dependency check, a container image's healthcheck, a form validator collecting every bad field. In book-of-composition this is the collect-all-failures shape again — and note that the partition is only as good as the guard: on the pre-2026-08-02 engine, where the miss was truthy, this same code would have reported every name present.")
    (:eng "State the parts you need as a list at the top of the file and check them in one call. Requirements you can read are requirements you can test."
     :math "The check is a fold over a predicate; totality of the predicate is what makes the fold meaningful."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a preflight gate — the same shape as a linker resolving every undefined symbol before it emits an executable.")))

(verb
  :name "lib-verbs" :std "Motoi"
  :library "standard-library"
  :kind "io"
  :signature "(lib-verbs part-name-symbol) -> list-of-symbols | #f"
  :summary "The list of verb names a standard part declares, as symbols, in the part's own declared order; #f if it is not a standard part."
  :provenance "lib/system/lib-tools.js:71; Motoi-own. Read 2026-08-02; miss value corrected from JavaScript null to #f the same day."
  :explanation "lib-verbs opens a part and reads out its VERBS declaration — the list of names the part promises to bind — as Scheme symbols. The order is the part's own source order, which is neither alphabetical nor grouped by kind; it is whatever the author wrote. The name lookup is the same one lib-info uses, exact first and case-insensitive second. A miss is #f, the same miss value lib-info gives. What lib-verbs gives you is a DECLARATION: it is the list of names the part said it would provide, not a scan of the environment, so a name here is a promise and lib-validate is what checks the promise was kept."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(length (lib-verbs 'SRFI-27))" :note "4 — the random-source part is the smallest one in the standard.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(lib-verbs 'SRFI-27)" :note "(make-random-source random-integer random-real random-source-randomize!) — source order, not alphabetical.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(and (memq 'string-contains (lib-verbs 'SRFI-13)) #t)" :note "#t — membership works because the names come back as real interned symbols.")
  )
  :caveats ("The order is the part's SOURCE order. Sort it yourself if you intend to compare two builds, or a reordering will read as a change." "A miss is #f, exactly as in lib-info — and note that #f is also what you get for a part that exists but was misspelled, so a #f here is a name problem, never an empty part." "This is a declaration, not a scan. A verb the part binds but did not declare is absent from this list, and a verb it declared but failed to bind is present in it." "Some names you expect in a part may live in R7RS-small instead — string-upcase, for example, is NOT in SRFI-13's list here. Check the core before concluding a name is missing.")
  :drawbacks ("No arity, no signature, no documentation — just names." "No way to ask the reverse question, 'which part does this verb come from', without scanning every part." "Rebuilds the symbol list on every call.")
  :usecases ("Checking that a specific verb is part of a specific part before relying on it." "Generating a per-part index for documentation." "Diffing two builds' surfaces part by part." "Answering a learner's 'what else is in the string library'.")
  :related ("lib-info" "std-parts" "lib-validate" "loaded-libs")
  :learn (:concept "A declared interface and an implemented one are different objects, and the interesting engineering lives in the gap between them." :prerequisites ("lib-info" "memq") :progression "Read the declaration with lib-verbs, then check it against reality with lib-validate.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (has-verb? part name)\n  (and (pair? (lib-verbs part)) (memq name (lib-verbs part)) #t))\n(display (has-verb? 'SRFI-13 'string-join)) (newline)\n(display (has-verb? 'SRFI-13 'string-upcase)) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (declared-vs-counted part)\n  (list (cadr (assq ':verb-count (lib-info part))) (length (lib-verbs part))))\n(display (declared-vs-counted 'SRFI-1)) (newline)\n(display (declared-vs-counted 'SRFI-125)) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (find-part name)\n  (let loop ((ps (std-parts)))\n    (cond ((null? ps) 'not-found)\n          ((memq name (lib-verbs (cadr (assq ':name (car ps)))))\n           (cadr (assq ':name (car ps))))\n          (else (loop (cdr ps))))))\n(display (find-part 'string-join)) (newline)\n(display (find-part 'fold)) (newline)"
     :problem "A learner is told string operations live in SRFI-13, looks for string-upcase there, does not find it, and concludes the build is broken. The audit question is the reverse of the one they asked: not 'is this verb in that part' but 'which part is this verb in'. The answer is that string-upcase is R7RS-small core, and the way to find that out is to scan every part rather than to guess."
     :explanation "eng: a membership predicate that survives a miss, because the pair? guard runs before the memq. math: the declared count and the measured length of the same declaration agree, which is the cheapest consistency check available. cs: the reverse index, built by scanning — the query the API does not offer, assembled out of the two it does."
     :meta "Why the second eng-program line prints #f: string-upcase is genuinely not in SRFI-13's declared list in this build; it is core. That is not a bug, it is the conformance ladder — R7RS-small takes a name first and the SRFI only carries what the core does not. The tell that this went wrong is a bug report about a missing verb that turns out to be bound and working the whole time.")
    (:eng "Before reporting a verb missing from a part, ask which part it IS in. The scan is nine lines and it settles the question."
     :math "Declared count and list length are two readings of one fact; disagreement means the registry is malformed."
     :cs "The CS pattern is building a reverse index from a forward one — the same move a symbol table makes."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The manifest of promises: a list that says what will be provided, kept separately from the thing that provides it, so the two can be compared."
     :rungs ((:domain "standard-library (a part's declared verb list, checkable against what it actually binds)" :code "(display (length (lib-verbs 'SRFI-125))) (newline)\n(display (cadr (assq ':verb-count (lib-info 'SRFI-125)))) (newline)")
             (:domain "shipping (a container manifest listing what is inside, read at the port before the doors open)" :code "(define manifest (list 'crates 'barrels 'rope))\n(display (length manifest)) (newline)\n(display (memq 'rope manifest)) (newline)")
             (:domain "formal specification (a signature declaring the operations a structure must support, before any model of it exists)" :code "(define signature (list 'zero 'add 'neg))\n(display (length signature)) (newline)\n(display (memq 'mul signature)) (newline)"))
     :explanation "A verb list, a cargo manifest, and an algebraic signature are the same artifact: a written promise about contents, produced before or apart from the contents themselves, whose whole value is that it can be checked against them. None of the three is worth anything on its own — the manifest that is never compared to the container is paperwork."
     :meta "The shape transfers wherever a promise and a delivery are separable. Where it breaks down: when the declaration IS the implementation — a language where the interface is generated from the code has no gap to check, and gains consistency at the cost of the ability to state an intent the code has not yet met.")
    (:eng "A manifest is only useful if something reads it against the cargo. Write the comparison, not just the list."
     :math "A signature constrains the models; it does not produce one."
     :cs "The CS analog is a header file, an interface definition, or a package's exports field — a promise separate from its fulfilment."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (length (lib-verbs 'SRFI-27))) (newline)\n(display (map (lambda (v) (symbol? v)) (lib-verbs 'SRFI-27))) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (agrees? p) (= (length (lib-verbs p)) (cadr (assq ':verb-count (lib-info p)))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (agrees? (cadr (assq ':name p)))) (std-parts))) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (equal? (lib-verbs 'srfi-27) (lib-verbs 'SRFI-27))) (newline)\n(display (pair? (lib-verbs 'nope))) (newline)"
     :claim "Every element is a symbol, the list length agrees with the part's declared verb-count for every part, and the name lookup case-folds exactly as lib-info's does."
     :invariant "∀p ∈ parts. |lib-verbs(p)| = verb-count(lib-info(p)) ∧ ∀v ∈ lib-verbs(p). symbol?(v)"
     :meta "The pattern is an internal-consistency proof: the two facts being compared come from the same source, so agreement proves the registry is well-formed and proves nothing at all about whether the verbs work. That second question is exactly what lib-validate's checks 3 and 5 exist for, and the split is deliberate — cheap structural checks here, expensive live checks there. What falsifies the naive reading: a reader who takes this as evidence the part is functional. It is not."
    )
    (:eng "Smoke test: element type, count agreement across every part, and case-folding."
     :math "Agreement of length and declared count is necessary for a well-formed registry, not sufficient for a working part."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a structural invariant check, deliberately separated from a behavioural one."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (index)\n  (map (lambda (p)\n         (let ((n (cadr (assq ':name p))))\n           (cons n (lib-verbs n))))\n       (std-parts)))\n(display (map car (index))) (newline)\n(display (length (cdr (assoc \"SRFI-27\" (index))))) (newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (total-declared)\n  (apply + (map (lambda (p) (length (lib-verbs (cadr (assq ':name p))))) (std-parts))))\n(display (total-declared)) (newline)\n(display (= (total-declared) (apply + (map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))) (std-parts))))) (newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (owner v)\n  (let loop ((ps (std-parts)))\n    (cond ((null? ps) 'core-or-motoi-own)\n          ((memq v (lib-verbs (cadr (assq ':name (car ps))))) (cadr (assq ':name (car ps))))\n          (else (loop (cdr ps))))))\n(display (map owner (list 'string-join 'random-real 'string-upcase))) (newline)"
     :base "lib-verbs reads one part's declared name list."
     :emergent "a provenance oracle — given any verb name, the part it came from, assembled by inverting five forward lists into one reverse lookup"
     :meta "The composition inverts a one-to-many map into a many-to-one one. What emerges is the attribution family: the ability to answer 'where did this come from' for anything in the language, which is the question every conformance argument starts with. Same family: a debugger mapping an address to a symbol, a linker mapping a symbol to an object file, a citation index mapping a claim to a paper. In book-of-composition this is the invert-the-index shape, and its characteristic tell is that the fallback case carries real information — a verb that belongs to no part is core or Motoi-own, which is an answer, not a failure.")
    (:eng "Build the reverse index once and keep it. Asking 'which part' repeatedly by scanning is fine for a handful of parts and wrong for fifty."
     :math "Inversion of a partial function needs a value for the empty preimage; here it is 'core-or-motoi-own', and it is informative."
     :cs "The CS pattern is symbol attribution — the same machinery behind stack-trace symbolication.")))

(verb
  :name "lib-validate" :std "Motoi"
  :library "standard-library"
  :kind "io"
  :signature "(lib-validate part-name-symbol) -> report-alist | #f"
  :summary "Runs the five-point gate on one standard part against the live environment and returns a report alist; #f if the name is not a standard part."
  :provenance "lib/system/lib-tools.js:77; validatePart in core/parts-registry.js. Motoi-own. Read 2026-08-02; miss value corrected from JavaScript null to #f the same day."
  :explanation "lib-validate is the gate from 00e's prose, callable from inside a program. It returns a four-entry alist: (:ok boolean), (:std string), (:spec string), and (:checks list). The checks list holds one entry per gate point, each a three-element list of the form (:id pass detail) — the id a keyword symbol, the pass a boolean, the detail a human-readable string such as \"part \\\"SRFI-27\\\" declares 4 verbs\". The ids come back in execution order, which is NOT numeric order: :1-bucket, :2-spec, :4-toggle, :3-verbs, :5-runs. The cheap structural checks run before the expensive live ones, and the numbering reflects the doctrine's order rather than the runner's. Validation happens against the RUNNING environment, so checks 3 and 5 are only meaningful for a part that is installed."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(cadr (assq ':ok (lib-validate 'SRFI-13)))" :note "#t — the string part passes all five points against the live environment.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(length (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate 'SRFI-27))))" :note "5 — one entry per gate point, always five.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(map car (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate 'SRFI-27))))" :note "(:1-bucket :2-spec :4-toggle :3-verbs :5-runs) — execution order, not numeric order.")
  )
  :caveats ("The check ids are returned in EXECUTION order, so :4-toggle arrives before :3-verbs. Sort by id if you are printing a numbered list, or the report will look wrong to a reader who knows the doctrine." "A miss returns #f, the same miss value lib-info returns — so a #f here means the part name is not in the registry, never that the gate failed. A failed gate is a report with (:ok #f) in it." "Checks 3 and 5 test the LIVE environment. A lazy part that has never been touched can still pass, because validation installs what it needs to look — do not read a pass as evidence the part was already loaded." "This validates STANDARD parts only. Validating an arbitrary library file is an async dynamic import and lives on the CLI as `motoi lib register`, not here.")
  :drawbacks ("No way to run a single check; it is all five or nothing." "The detail strings are human-readable prose, not structured data — parsing them is a trap." "Cost scales with the part's verb count, because check 3 touches every declared name." "A failure tells you which point failed, not how to fix it.")
  :usecases ("Asserting in a test that the standard library is intact before running a suite." "Diagnosing an unbound verb by asking whether its part still validates." "Checking a part after a build change that touched the registry." "Teaching the five-point gate by showing its output rather than describing it.")
  :related ("lib-validate-all" "lib-info" "lib-verbs" "std-parts")
  :learn (:concept "A gate is a fixed list of checks that all must pass; publishing the per-check result is what makes a failure diagnosable instead of merely fatal." :prerequisites ("lib-info" "lib-verbs") :progression "Read a part with lib-info, its promises with lib-verbs, then check the promises with lib-validate.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (gate part)\n  (let ((r (lib-validate part)))\n    (list part (cadr (assq ':ok r)) (length (cadr (assq ':checks r))))))\n(display (gate 'SRFI-1)) (newline)\n(display (gate 'SRFI-125)) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (all-pass? part)\n  (let loop ((cs (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate part)))))\n    (cond ((null? cs) #t)\n          ((cadr (car cs)) (loop (cdr cs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(display (all-pass? 'SRFI-27)) (newline)\n(display (eq? (all-pass? 'SRFI-27) (cadr (assq ':ok (lib-validate 'SRFI-27))))) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (failed part)\n  (let loop ((cs (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate part)))) (acc '()))\n    (cond ((null? cs) (reverse acc))\n          ((cadr (car cs)) (loop (cdr cs) acc))\n          (else (loop (cdr cs) (cons (car (car cs)) acc))))))\n(display (failed 'SRFI-13)) (newline)"
     :problem "A verb from a lazy part is unbound at runtime and the operator wants to know whether the part is broken or was simply never reached. The audit question is which of the five points fails — a spec-file problem and a binding problem are different bugs with different fixes, and :ok alone cannot tell them apart. All three programs go past the summary boolean into the per-check list, which is the only place the answer lives."
     :explanation "eng: the summary line an operator reads, part plus verdict plus check count. math: the summary boolean is derived from the checks and agrees with a fold over them, which is the property that lets you trust :ok when you are in a hurry. cs: the failure set — empty on a healthy part, and exactly the diagnosis on a broken one."
     :meta "Why not stop at :ok: because a red build gives you one bit and you need five. Why the ids and not the detail strings: the ids are stable symbols and the details are prose that will be reworded. The tell that this went wrong is a bug report that says 'lib-validate failed' with no id attached, which costs a round trip every single time.")
    (:eng "Log failed check IDS, never the detail prose. Ids are an interface; prose is not."
     :math "The summary is a conjunction over the checks; verify that relationship once and then trust the summary."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a composite health check that publishes its components. Related: readiness probes, preflight suites, fsck."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The gate with published sub-results: a pass/fail decision that also hands back the reason, so a failure is a diagnosis rather than a door."
     :rungs ((:domain "standard-library (five checks per part, each reported with its own id and verdict)" :code "(display (map car (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate 'SRFI-1))))) (newline)\n(display (map cadr (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate 'SRFI-1))))) (newline)")
             (:domain "aviation (a pre-flight checklist where every line is initialled, so a hold names the item)" :code "(define checks (list (list 'fuel #t) (list 'flaps #t) (list 'radio #t)))\n(display (map car checks)) (newline)\n(display (map cadr checks)) (newline)")
             (:domain "proof checking (a conjunction whose falsity is witnessed by a specific conjunct)" :code "(define claims (list #t #t #f))\n(define (witness cs)\n  (let loop ((xs cs) (i 0))\n    (cond ((null? xs) 'none) ((car xs) (loop (cdr xs) (+ i 1))) (else i))))\n(display (witness claims)) (newline)"))
     :explanation "A five-point gate, a pre-flight checklist, and a witnessed conjunction are one structure: an AND over named conditions, where the value is not in the AND but in knowing WHICH conjunct was false. A checklist that only reported 'not ready' would ground the aircraft without telling anyone why, and that is precisely the useless artifact each of these is designed not to be."
     :meta "The shape transfers to every composite check. Where it does not: when the checks are not independent — a cascading gate where check 3 is meaningless if check 2 failed cannot report a flat list of verdicts honestly, and must report 'not run' as a third state rather than pretending a skipped check passed.")
    (:eng "Never publish a bare verdict for a composite check. The sub-results are the product; the verdict is a convenience."
     :math "A false conjunction has a witness; report the witness, not the conjunction."
     :cs "The CS analog is a health endpoint returning per-dependency status rather than a single up/down bit."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (map car (lib-validate 'SRFI-125))) (newline)\n(display (length (lib-validate 'SRFI-125))) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (fold-checks part)\n  (let loop ((cs (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate part)))))\n    (cond ((null? cs) #t) ((cadr (car cs)) (loop (cdr cs))) (else #f))))\n(display (map (lambda (p) (eq? (fold-checks (cadr (assq ':name p)))\n                               (cadr (assq ':ok (lib-validate (cadr (assq ':name p)))))))\n              (std-parts))) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(display (map (lambda (c) (length c)) (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate 'SRFI-27))))) (newline)\n(display (pair? (lib-validate 'nope))) (newline)"
     :claim "The report has exactly four top-level keys, every check entry is a three-element (id pass detail) list, and the :ok summary equals the conjunction of the check verdicts for every one of the parts."
     :invariant "∀p ∈ parts. ok(p) ⇔ ⋀ᵢ passᵢ(p) ∧ |checks(p)| = 5 ∧ ∀c. |c| = 3"
     :meta "The pattern is proving the summary is not independent evidence — :ok carries no information the checks do not already carry, which is what makes it safe to short-circuit on. The math-program checks that equivalence across every part rather than one, because a summary that agreed on one part and diverged on another would be the worst possible defect: a green build that is green for the wrong reason. What falsifies the naive reading: nothing here proves the CHECKS are the right checks. That is a doctrine question, settled in 00e's prose, not a runtime one."
    )
    (:eng "Smoke test: report shape, per-check shape, and summary-equals-conjunction across every part."
     :math "The equivalence is what licenses short-circuiting on the summary; verify it, then use it."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a derived aggregate proved consistent with its components."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (diagnose part)\n  (let* ((r (lib-validate part))\n         (bad (let loop ((cs (cadr (assq ':checks r))) (acc '()))\n                (cond ((null? cs) (reverse acc))\n                      ((cadr (car cs)) (loop (cdr cs) acc))\n                      (else (loop (cdr cs) (cons (car (car cs)) acc)))))))\n    (if (null? bad) (list part 'healthy) (list part 'failed bad))))\n(display (diagnose 'SRFI-13)) (newline)\n(display (diagnose 'SRFI-27)) (newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (health)\n  (map (lambda (p) (cadr (assq ':ok (lib-validate (cadr (assq ':name p)))))) (std-parts)))\n(display (health)) (newline)\n(display (let loop ((xs (health))) (cond ((null? xs) #t) ((car xs) (loop (cdr xs))) (else #f)))) (newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (detail-of part id)\n  (let loop ((cs (cadr (assq ':checks (lib-validate part)))))\n    (cond ((null? cs) 'no-such-check)\n          ((eq? (car (car cs)) id) (caddr (car cs)))\n          (else (loop (cdr cs))))))\n(display (detail-of 'SRFI-27 ':1-bucket)) (newline)\n(display (detail-of 'SRFI-27 ':9-nope)) (newline)"
     :base "lib-validate runs five checks on one part and reports each."
     :emergent "a standard-library self-test — the language proving, from inside a running program, that every part it claims to ship is structurally sound and behaviourally live, with a named reason for any part that is not"
     :meta "The composition lifts a per-part gate over the whole registry and then drills back down into a single named check. What emerges is the self-test family: a system that can assert its own integrity without an external harness. Same family: a filesystem's fsck, a database's integrity_check, a CPU's power-on self test, a compiler bootstrapping itself and comparing stages. In book-of-composition this is the fixed-point-of-inspection shape — the system is both the subject and the instrument, and the interesting failure mode is that a sufficiently broken system cannot report its own brokenness, which is why the cheap structural checks run first.")
    (:eng "Put the whole-registry health call in the test suite. A standard library that cannot self-test is trusted rather than known."
     :math "Lifting a predicate over an index set gives a conjunction; keeping the per-element witness is what makes it debuggable."
     :cs "The CS pattern is power-on self test — and its known limit is that the tester shares fate with the tested.")))

(verb
  :name "lib-validate-all" :std "Motoi"
  :library "standard-library"
  :kind "io"
  :signature "(lib-validate-all) -> list-of-tagged-reports"
  :summary "Runs the five-point gate on every standard part and returns one name-tagged report per part."
  :provenance "lib/system/lib-tools.js:84; Motoi-own. Read 2026-08-02."
  :explanation "lib-validate-all is lib-validate lifted over the whole registry. It returns one entry per part, in registry order, and each entry is the part's report alist with the part NAME pushed on the front as a keyword symbol — so an entry is a five-element list whose car is :SRFI-27 and whose cdr is the ordinary ((:ok …) (:std …) (:spec …) (:checks …)) report. That shape is worth stating precisely, because it is not a pair of name and report and it is not an alist entry: the tag is consed onto the report, so (car e) is the tag and (cdr e) is a report you can hand straight to assq. There is no argument and no way to validate a subset; it is every part, every time."
  :examples (
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "novice" :code "(length (lib-validate-all))" :note "7 — one report per standard part, always.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "intermediate" :code "(map car (lib-validate-all))" :note "(:R7RS-small :SRFI-1 :SRFI-13 :SRFI-125 :SRFI-27 :SRFI-48 :SRFI-133) — the tag is a keyword symbol, not a string.")
    (:dialect "motoi" :tier "expert" :code "(map (lambda (r) (cadr (assq ':ok (cdr r)))) (lib-validate-all))" :note "(#t #t #t #t #t #t #t) — a healthy standard library, part by part.")
  )
  :caveats ("The entry is the report with the tag CONSED ON, not a (name . report) pair. Use (cdr e) to get something assq can read; (cadr e) gives you the (:ok …) entry, which is a different thing and looks close enough to be confusing." "The tag is a keyword symbol like :SRFI-27, while lib-info's :name entry is the STRING \"SRFI-27\". Joining the two tables needs a conversion." "No argument, no subset. Every call revalidates every part, and check 3 touches every declared verb in each — this is not a verb to call in a loop." "Registry order, not alphabetical. R7RS-small is first because it is the core, not because of its letters.")
  :drawbacks ("All-or-nothing: no way to skip an expensive part." "No aggregate verdict is returned; the caller folds the :ok values themselves." "The tagged-list shape is unusual enough that it invites off-by-one accessor bugs." "Cost grows with the standard library, so a suite that calls it per test will feel it.")
  :usecases ("A one-line assertion in a test suite that the whole standard library is intact." "A post-build gate after any change to the parts registry." "Producing the same table `motoi lib validate` prints, from inside a program." "Catching a part that regressed without knowing in advance which one to suspect.")
  :related ("lib-validate" "std-parts" "lib-info" "lib-verbs")
  :learn (:concept "Lifting a single-subject check over a whole collection turns a diagnostic into a gate; keeping the per-subject result is what stops it becoming a single useless bit." :prerequisites ("lib-validate") :progression "Learn lib-validate on one part, then lib-validate-all as the suite, then fold the results into the one boolean a build script wants.")
  :row-2-audit (
    (:eng-program "(define (summary)\n  (map (lambda (r) (list (car r) (cadr (assq ':ok (cdr r))))) (lib-validate-all)))\n(display (summary)) (newline)"
     :math-program "(define (green?)\n  (let loop ((rs (lib-validate-all)))\n    (cond ((null? rs) #t)\n          ((cadr (assq ':ok (cdr (car rs)))) (loop (cdr rs)))\n          (else #f))))\n(display (green?)) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (red-parts)\n  (let loop ((rs (lib-validate-all)) (acc '()))\n    (cond ((null? rs) (reverse acc))\n          ((cadr (assq ':ok (cdr (car rs)))) (loop (cdr rs) acc))\n          (else (loop (cdr rs) (cons (car (car rs)) acc))))))\n(display (red-parts)) (newline)"
     :problem "A build script needs one answer — ship or do not ship — but a maintainer staring at a red build needs a different one: which part, and which check inside it. The audit question is how to serve both from one call without running the expensive gate twice. The answer is to call once, keep the full tagged list, and derive the boolean from it rather than asking for the boolean."
     :explanation "eng: the operator's table, tag plus verdict, one line per part. math: the single boolean a build script exits on, folded from the same data. cs: the list of failing tags, which is empty on a green build and is the entire bug report on a red one."
     :meta "Why derive the boolean instead of asking for one: the verb does not offer an aggregate, and that is the right call — an API that only returned the aggregate would force a second full run to find out which part failed. The tell that this went wrong is a CI log that says the standard library is broken and nothing else, which is a whole-registry search the machine already did and threw away.")
    (:eng "Call it once, keep the whole list, derive every view you need from it. Re-running a gate to get a different summary is paying twice for one fact."
     :math "The aggregate is a fold over the parts; the fold is cheap, the data is not."
     :cs "The CS pattern is fan-out health checking with per-subject retention. Related: test-suite reporters, multi-service readiness probes."))
  :row-3-dimension (
    (:setup "The census: one identical examination applied to every member of a population at once, keeping each result rather than only the total."
     :rungs ((:domain "standard-library (the five-point gate run over every part in one call)" :code "(display (map car (lib-validate-all))) (newline)\n(display (map (lambda (r) (cadr (assq ':ok (cdr r)))) (lib-validate-all))) (newline)")
             (:domain "public health (a screening programme run across a whole cohort, with individual results retained)" :code "(define cohort (list (list 'a 'clear) (list 'b 'clear) (list 'c 'flagged)))\n(display (map car cohort)) (newline)\n(display (map cadr cohort)) (newline)")
             (:domain "universal quantification (a claim over a domain, whose refutation is a specific counterexample)" :code "(define xs (list 2 4 6 7))\n(define (even-all? l)\n  (let loop ((ys l)) (cond ((null? ys) 'none) ((even? (car ys)) (loop (cdr ys))) (else (car ys)))))\n(display (even-all? xs)) (newline)"))
     :explanation "A parts gate, a screening programme, and a universally quantified claim are the same operation seen from three sides: apply one test to every element, and — crucially — keep the identity of the elements that failed. A census that reported only a percentage would be the same waste as a gate that reported only a boolean, and a ∀ claim without its counterexample is a claim you cannot act on."
     :meta "The shape transfers whenever the test is uniform and the population is enumerable. Where it fails: when the test is expensive and the population is large, at which point sampling replaces census and the guarantee weakens from 'all' to 'probably all' — the registry is small enough that the census is free and the weaker guarantee would be a bad trade.")
    (:eng "Keep identities, not just totals. A count of failures is a number; a list of them is a work queue."
     :math "The refutation of a universal claim is a witness; a census retains every witness by construction."
     :cs "The CS analog is a test runner reporting per-test results rather than a pass count."))
  :row-4-proof (
    (:eng-program "(display (length (lib-validate-all))) (newline)\n(display (map length (lib-validate-all))) (newline)"
     :math-program "(display (equal? (map car (lib-validate-all))\n                 (map car (lib-validate-all)))) (newline)\n(display (map (lambda (r) (length (cadr (assq ':checks (cdr r))))) (lib-validate-all))) (newline)"
     :cs-program "(define (via-all p)\n  (let loop ((rs (lib-validate-all)))\n    (cond ((null? rs) 'missing)\n          ((eq? (car (car rs)) p) (cadr (assq ':ok (cdr (car rs)))))\n          (else (loop (cdr rs))))))\n(display (via-all ':SRFI-27)) (newline)\n(display (eq? (via-all ':SRFI-27) (cadr (assq ':ok (lib-validate 'SRFI-27))))) (newline)"
     :claim "One report per part comes back, each a five-element tagged list carrying five checks, and a part's verdict read out of lib-validate-all is the same verdict lib-validate gives for that part alone."
     :invariant "|validate-all()| = |std-parts()| ∧ ∀e. |e| = 5 ∧ |checks(cdr e)| = 5 ∧ ok(cdr eₚ) = ok(lib-validate(p))"
     :meta "The pattern is proving a lifted operation agrees with its point-wise original — the only property that makes the batch form substitutable for the single form. Without it, a green batch and a red singleton could coexist and neither would be wrong. What falsifies the naive reading: the tag comparison needs the KEYWORD :SRFI-27 while lib-validate takes the plain symbol SRFI-27, and that asymmetry is real, not an artifact of how the test is written. See the caveat on tag types."
    )
    (:eng "Smoke test: count, entry shape, checks-per-entry, and agreement with the single-part form."
     :math "Lifted and point-wise must agree elementwise, or the batch form is not a shorthand but a second implementation."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a batch API verified against its singular counterpart."))
  :row-5-emergence (
    (:eng-composition "(define (report)\n  (map (lambda (r)\n         (let* ((tag (car r))\n                (checks (cadr (assq ':checks (cdr r))))\n                (bad (let loop ((cs checks) (acc '()))\n                       (cond ((null? cs) (reverse acc))\n                             ((cadr (car cs)) (loop (cdr cs) acc))\n                             (else (loop (cdr cs) (cons (car (car cs)) acc)))))))\n           (if (null? bad) (list tag 'pass) (list tag 'fail bad))))\n       (lib-validate-all)))\n(display (report)) (newline)"
     :math-composition "(define (verdicts) (map (lambda (r) (cadr (assq ':ok (cdr r)))) (lib-validate-all)))\n(define (green? vs) (let loop ((xs vs)) (cond ((null? xs) #t) ((car xs) (loop (cdr xs))) (else #f))))\n(display (verdicts)) (newline)\n(display (green? (verdicts))) (newline)\n(display (green? (list #t #f #t))) (newline)"
     :cs-composition "(define (join)\n  (map (lambda (p)\n         (let ((n (cadr (assq ':name p))))\n           (list n (cadr (assq ':mode p)) (cadr (assq ':verb-count p))\n                 (cadr (assq ':ok (lib-validate n))))))\n       (std-parts)))\n(display (join)) (newline)"
     :base "lib-validate-all runs the gate across the registry and tags each report."
     :emergent "a release gate — one call that decides whether a build of the language may ship, and, if not, hands over the part, the failing check ids, and the metadata needed to open the right file"
     :meta "The composition joins the validation results back against the registry metadata, producing the single table a release engineer actually wants: name, mode, size, verdict. What emerges is the ship-decision family — a derived artifact whose whole purpose is to compress a large investigation into one boolean WITHOUT discarding the investigation. Same family: a CI status check with an expandable log, a pre-merge gate linking to the failing job, an aircraft dispatch decision backed by a deferred-defects list. In book-of-composition this is the summarize-but-retain shape, and its discipline is a rule: the boolean must always be derivable from the retained detail, never the other way round.")
    (:eng "One gate call, one table, one boolean — and keep the table. The boolean is what the script reads; the table is what the person reads."
     :math "The verdict is a fold; the retained per-part results are the witnesses that make a false fold actionable."
     :cs "The CS pattern is a release gate over a component matrix, the same shape as a compatibility table in a CI dashboard.")))
